<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zograf Monastery</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Zografou</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zograf Monastery is the Bulgarian monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, traditionally founded in the late 10th century and dedicated to Saint George. Its name, 'the painter', comes from a legend of an icon that painted itself. It has long been a centre of Bulgarian culture: there in 1762 the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paisius-of-hilendar">Paisius of Hilendar</a> completed his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonic-bulgarian-history">Slavonic-Bulgarian History</a>, which helped begin the Bulgarian national revival.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zograf-monastery.jpg" alt="Zograf Monastery" width="320" height="241" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Georgid · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zograf_Monastery.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Zograf Monastery, named for Saint George the Zograf, is one of the twenty Eastern Orthodox monasteries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a>. It was founded by three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohrid">Ohrid</a> and is regarded as the historical Bulgarian monastery on Athos, traditionally inhabited by Bulgarian Orthodox monks. The earliest written evidence of it dates from 980. The monastery takes its name from a 13th- or 14th-century icon of Saint George. Zograf means painter in Greek, and the icon is believed to have painted itself on the prepared board.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Bulgarian rulers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-asen-ii">Ivan Asen II</a> and Ivan Alexander supported the monastery generously, since it was a matter of pride for the Bulgarian Orthodox Church to keep a house on Athos. It also received land from Byzantine rulers, the first being <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-vi-the-wise">Leo VI the Wise</a>, and from Serbian rulers. In 1275 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> acting on the orders of the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII Palaiologos</a> plundered and burned it and killed 26 monks, including the abbot Thomas. The cause was the opposition of the Athonite monks to the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyons</a>, which the emperor supported for political reasons. Their martyrdom is commemorated each year on 10 October.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">Mercenaries</a> of the Catalan Grand Company raided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Holy Mountain</a> in 1307–9, and of some 300 monasteries on Athos at the start of the 14th century only 35 were left by the end. Zograf recovered quickly with grants from the Palaeologue emperors and the princes of Moldavia and Wallachia, and its buildings were reconstructed with the aid of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andronikos-ii-palaiologos">Andronicus II Palaeologus</a>. The present buildings date from the middle of the 18th century: the south wing was built in 1750, the east in 1758, the small church in 1764 and the large one in 1801. Large-scale construction ended in 1896.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The monastery library is of major importance to Bulgarian culture. It holds 388 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> in Church Slavonic, 126 in Greek and about 10,000 printed books, among them the original draft of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paisius-of-hilendar">Paisius of Hilendar</a>'s Istoriya Slavyanobolgarska. Two medieval Bulgarian royal charters, the Zograf Charter and the Rila Charter, were found there. The monastery and its seal appear on the Bulgarian 2 lev banknote issued in 1999 and 2005, and a 10 lev <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coin of 2011 features it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zograf Monastery” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Twenty ruling monasteries, Greek, Serbian (Hilandar), Russian (Saint Panteleimon), Bulgarian (<b>Zograf</b>) and others, own the land and send representatives to the Holy Community at Karyes.</blockquote><a href="/state/monastic-community-of-mount-athos">Monastic Community of Mount Athos</a></li></ul></section>
